<Project>
  <!-- Import parent props first -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <!--
    A metapackage's whole job is to name the handful of packages it bundles. Central transitive pinning
    promotes the UNION of every referenced project's transitive graph onto the nuspec as DIRECT
    dependencies, which is harmless for an implementation package (it has real content, so the promoted
    set is close to what it already uses) and wrong for a project whose entire content is
    ProjectReferences: the bundle list disappears into the graph it bundles.

    Measured on Excalibur.Postgres before this file existed: 60 direct dependencies for a package that
    references 7 projects and 2 packages, including QuestPDF, MongoDB.Driver and Npgsql. A consumer
    installing a package named for Postgres pulled a PDF library and the MongoDB driver into their app.

    Turning pinning off here does NOT lose the version floors it was providing. Those floors live on the
    implementation packages, which keep pinning and keep declaring them, and a consumer of a metapackage
    reaches them through exactly that chain.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The opt-out above only survives because the central package management file guards its own setter
    with a condition on the property being unset. That file is imported after this one, so removing the
    condition there would overwrite the opt-out while leaving it looking applied: the property is still
    written here, the file still reads correctly, and every metapackage silently regains the promoted
    union of its referenced projects' transitive graphs.

    Reading the property back at build time is the only check that cannot be fooled by that, because it
    reads the value the build actually used rather than the text of either file.
  -->
  <Target Name="VerifyMetapackageTransitivePinningIsOff" BeforeTargets="Build;Pack">
    <Error Condition="'$(CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled)' != 'false'"
           Text="Metapackage $(MSBuildProjectName) built with CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled='$(CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled)', expected 'false'. Something imported after this file overwrote the opt-out, and packing now would list the union of every referenced project's transitive dependencies as direct dependencies of this bundle. Restore the condition on the setter in the central package management file." />
  </Target>
</Project>
